```
Reason:
The first statement attempts to add a cluster without checking if the cluster is fully set up, which can lead to the Ambari Metrics Service (AMS) stopping if the cluster is not ready. The second statement tries to get instances from the cluster right after, which can fail if the cluster is still being initialized, leading to a failure in adding instances.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
boolean clusterAdded = admin.addCluster(clusterName, false);
LOG.info("Was cluster added successfully? " + clusterAdded);
```
```java
for (int i = 0; i < tries && !success; i++) {
    try {
        List<String> nodes = admin.getInstancesInCluster(clusterName);
        ...
    } catch (HelixException | ZkNoNodeException ex) {
        LOG.warn("Helix Cluster not yet setup fully.");
```
Reason:
The first statement captures whether the cluster was added successfully and logs this information, allowing for better diagnosis of the setup process. The second set of statements introduces a retry mechanism that waits for the cluster to be fully set up before attempting to get instances and add them, thus preventing the AMS from stopping due to premature actions when the cluster is not ready.

